title:: Short Sound-File Playback
summary:: A practical guide to short sound-file playback, covering buffer loading and querying, UGen selection, looping, rate-controlled playback (including reverse), fade-based duration control, safe node cleanup, channel-mismatch handling, and folder-based bulk buffer loading.
categories:: Guides
related:: Classes/Buffer, Classes/UGen, Classes/PlayBuf, Classes/BufRd, Classes/BufRateScale, Classes/BufFrames, Classes/Line, Classes/Phasor, Classes/Delay1, Classes/Done, Classes/Env, Classes/EnvGen, Classes/Pan2, Classes/Balance2, Classes/Splay, Classes/SynthDef, Classes/Synth, Classes/SoundFile#*collectIntoBuffers, Classes/Node#-free, Classes/Node#-release, Classes/FreeSelf, Classes/Routine, Classes/Task, Classes/Server#-sync, Classes/BufDur, Classes/BufSampleRate, Guides/Non-Realtime-Synthesis

note::
list::
## For long sound files,
disk-based streaming is usually the better choice.
See link::Classes/Buffer#*cueSoundFile::,
link::Classes/DiskIn::,
and link::Classes/VDiskIn::.
## For granular synthesis,
see link::Classes/GrainBuf::,
link::Classes/GrainIn::,
link::Classes/GrainFM::,
link::Classes/GrainSin::,
and link::Classes/TGrains::.
::
::

description::

This guide covers short sound-file playback in SuperCollider,
using link::Classes/Buffer:: with link::Classes/PlayBuf:: and link::Classes/BufRd::, including:

list::
## Allocating link::Classes/Buffer::s using short sound sound files,
with or without selecting,
reordering
or duplicating channels
(code::Buffer.read:: vs code::Buffer.readChannel::).
## inspecting buffer properties:
bufnum, frames, channels, sample rate, path, and duration.
## choosing the appropriate playback primitive:
link::Classes/PlayBuf:: for higher-level playback,
link::Classes/BufRd:: for explicit phase control.
## implementing one-shot playback and looping,
including correct code::doneAction:: behavior.
## changing rate (playback speed and direction),
while accounting for duration and buffer/server sample-rate differences.
## reverse playback strategies,
including negative rates and appropriate start positions.
## applying fades and reliably freeing link::Classes/Synth:: instances
(which exist as link::Classes/Node::s on the server) created via
list::
## link::Classes/Function#-play::: code::{ ... }.play::
## link::Classes/Synth#*new::: code::Synth.new(...):: or the shorthand code::Synth(...)::
::
## handling channel-count mismatches between buffers and output link::Classes/UGen::s,
including panning, balancing, and downmixing.
## making buffer banks from folders,
including recursive folder structures.
::

note::
Except for the section link::#Practical Examples::,
the code samples are intentionally minimal,
so beginners can learn one concept at a time,
and experts can scan quickly.
::

section:: Short Sound-File Buffer Setup: Allocating and Querying

Allocating buffers on a server is asynchronous also when using short sound files. (See link::Guides/Sync-Async:: for basics and link::#Asynchronous Buffer Allocation:: for the cases in this guide.)
For short sound files,
the most common entry points are:

list::
## Class methods link::Classes/Buffer#*read:: and link::Classes/Buffer#*readChannel::,

which allocates a new buffer and reads an entire sound file, or selected parts of it, into the buffer.
## Instance methods link::Classes/Buffer#-read:: and link::Classes/Buffer#-readChannel::,

which read an entire sound file, or selected parts of it, into an already allocated buffer.
::

table::
##
Terms for Beginners:

definitionlist::
## Class Methods
|| Used to create instances of a class, or to manage all instances of that class.
Prefixed with * when specified.

For example link::Classes/Buffer#*read::
## Instance Methods
|| Used to operate on or control a specific existing instance of a class.
Prefixed with - when specified.

For example link::Classes/Buffer#-read::
::

See also:
link::Reference/Classes#Class Methods:: and
link::Reference/Classes#Instance Methods::
::

subsection:: Buffers: Buffer.read vs Buffer.readChannel

definitionlist::
## code::Buffer.read::
|| Reads the entire file,
preserving the file’s native channel count and channel order.
## code::Buffer.readChannel::
|| Reads only the specified channel(s).
You can select channels using 0-based indices,
reorder them by changing the index order,
or duplicate them by repeating indices.
note::
Duplicating a mono channel into a stereo buffer uses roughly twice the memory
of loading it as mono.
::
::

subsubsection:: Arguments for Buffer.read and Buffer.readChannel

note::
The signatures below show positional argument order,
but arguments can also be passed by keyword, regardless of positional order.
::

Both class methods accept the same core arguments,
except that code::Buffer.readChannel:: adds the code::channels:: argument.

table::
## code::
Buffer.read
(server, path, startFrame: 0, numFrames: -1, action, bufnum)

Buffer.readChannel
(server, path, startFrame: 0, numFrames: -1, channels, action, bufnum)
::
::

Instance methods have a different signature.
They read into an existing Buffer instance,
so there is no code::server:: argument,
and they add code::bufStartFrame:: and code::leaveOpen::.

Also, both instance methods accept the same core arguments,
except that code::Buffer.readChannel:: adds the code::channels:: argument.

table::
## code::
Buffer.read
(argpath, fileStartFrame: 0, numFrames: -1, bufStartFrame: 0, leaveOpen: false, action)

Buffer.readChannel
(argpath, fileStartFrame: 0, numFrames: -1, bufStartFrame: 0, leaveOpen: false, channels, action)
::
::

subsubsection:: Class method arguments (Buffer.read / Buffer.readChannel)

list::
## code::server:: (link::Classes/Server::)

The server on which to allocate and load the buffer.
Most examples use the default interpreter variable code::s::, which refers to the link::Classes/Server#*local#local (localhost)::, link::Classes/Server#*default#default server::.

## code::path:: (link::Classes/String::)

Path to the sound file.
Folder should full path including file name and extension.

## code::startFrame:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

The first frame in the sound file to read.
Frame indices are 0-based.
Floating-point values are truncated to integers.

## code::numFrames:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

Number of 0-based frames to read.
code::-1:: reads to the end of the file.
Floating-point values are truncated to integers.
## code::channels:: (link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Integer::)
⇦ Only for code::Buffer.readChannel::

An array of channel indices to read,
in the order provided.
Indices start at 0.
You may repeat indices to duplicate channels.

The number of requested channels must match this buffer’s code::numChannels::.
## code::action:: (link::Classes/Function::)

A function evaluated after the client (sclang) receives the server’s completion (allocating a buffer on server and loading a sound file on the buffer) reply,
and after the Buffer’s instance variables have been updated.
It receives the Buffer as its argument.

## code::bufnum:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

An explicitly specified buffer number.
Supplying a number bypasses the server’s buffer allocator,
which can lead to conflicts.
Best practice is to omit this argument,
and let the allocator choose a free buffer number.
::

subsubsection:: Instance method arguments (Buffer:read / Buffer:readChannel)

The instance methods read into an already allocated buffer,
so there is no code::server:: argument.
The order of code::startFrame:: and code::numFrames:: is reversed.
They also have two arguments which are not present in class methodds:

list::
## code::argpath:: (link::Classes/String::)

This corresponds to code::path:: argument in code::Buffer.read:: and code::Buffer.readChannel::.

## code::fileStartFrame:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

This corresponds to code::startFrame:: argument in code::Buffer.read:: and code::Buffer.readChannel::.

## code::numFrames:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

This corresponds to code::numFrames:: argument in code::Buffer.read:: and code::Buffer.readChannel::.

## code::bufStartFrame:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

The starting frame index in the buffer,
where the sound file data will be placed when reading.
Floating-point values are truncated to integers.

## code::leaveOpen:: (link::Classes/Boolean::)

Whether to leave the buffer open.
This is mainly used for disk streaming use cases,
for example with link::Classes/DiskIn::.
The default is code::false::,
which is correct for typical short-file playback.

## code::channels:: (link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Integer::)
⇦ Only for code::.readChannel(... )::

This corresponds to code::channels:: argument in code::Buffer.readChannel::.

## code::action:: (link::Classes/Function::)

This corresponds to code::action:: argument in code::Buffer.read:: and code::Buffer.readChannel::.

## code::completionMessage:: (OSC message or link::Classes/Function::)

An OSC completion message to run on the server,
or a function that returns one.
If a function is used,
it is evaluated in the language with the Buffer as its argument.
::

subsubsection:: Asynchronous Buffer Allocation

code::Buffer.read:: and code::Buffer.readChannel:: load sound files asynchronously.
This means the buffer may not be ready immediately after the call returns,
even if the Post window prints messages that look like loading has completed.

For example:

code::
Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.apollo11);
::

Immediately after evaluating the line above,
the Buffer’s printed representation may show only code::bufnum:: (the first value 0 below),
while other fields are still unknown:

teletype::
-> Buffer(0, nil, nil, nil, path-to-sounds/a11wlk01-44_1.aiff)
::

To run code as soon as loading finishes,
use one of the following approaches:
list::
## Use the code::action:: argument to run code after the buffer has finished loading. It should be a link::Classes/Function:: instance:
code::
(
Buffer.read(
	s,
	ExampleFiles.apollo11,
	action: { |buffer|
		buffer.play  // plays the loaded buffer.
	}
);
)
::

## If you load buffers inside a time-scheduled link::Classes/Routine::
or link::Classes/Task::
(or if you use related shortcuts such as code::fork { ... }:: and code::s.waitForBoot { ... }::),
call code::s.sync:: before you play or query the buffer.
This blocks the language
until the server has processed pending load requests.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot {
	var buf = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.apollo11);
	s.sync;
	buf.play;
};
)
::
## If you use instance methods, code::completonMessage:: could be also used:
code::
(
fork {
b = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.apollo11, action: { |buf| buf.play; ("bufnum:" + buf;).postln });

s.sync;

b.read(ExampleFiles.child, completionMessage: { |buf| ("bufnum:" + buf).postln });
}
)
::
::

subsubsection:: Loading Sound Files to Buffers Used in This Guide

code::
(
~b_numCh1 = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.apollo11);
~b_numCh2 = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.sinedPink);
~b_mono = Buffer.readChannel(s, ExampleFiles.child, channels: [0]);
~b_stereo_duped = Buffer.readChannel(s, ExampleFiles.child, channels: [0, 0]);
)
::

subsection:: Querying Buffer Metadata

A code::Buffer:: object lives in the language (sclang),
but it represents a buffer allocated on the server.
Internally,
the language uses values such as code::bufnum::
(the server-side buffer number)
to address the correct server resource.

In everyday code,
you usually do not pass code::bufnum:: around directly.
Instead,
keep a language-side variable,
for example, code::~b_numCh1::,
and query properties via methods such as code::bufnum::,
code::numChannels::,
and code::numFrames::.

subsubsection:: Querying Buffers Used in This Guide

The following examples show how to query buffer properties
and use code::.class:: to check their data types, as represented by link::Classes/Class::.

code::
~b_numCh1.query;             // Print a report.
~b_numCh1.query.class;       // -> Buffer

~b_numCh1.bufnum;            // Server buffer number (bufnum).
~b_numCh1.bufnum.class;      // -> Integer

~b_numCh1.numFrames;         // Number of frames.
~b_numCh1.numFrames.class;   // -> Integer

~b_numCh1.numChannels;       // Number of channels.
~b_numCh1.numChannels.class; // -> Integer

~b_numCh1.sampleRate;        // Sample rate.
~b_numCh1.sampleRate.class;  // -> Float

~b_numCh1.duration;          // Duration in seconds.
~b_numCh1.duration.class;    // -> Float

~b_numCh1.path;              // Path of the sound file loaded into the buffer.
~b_numCh1.path.class;        // -> String
::

note::
Duration in seconds:
list::
## In sclang: code::frames / buffer.sampleRate::
## On the server: code::BufFrames.kr(bufnum) / BufSampleRate.kr(bufnum)::
::
::

subsubsection:: Visual Comfirmation of Loaded Buffer by Plotting

Overviewing the buffer's waveform is possible by link::Classes/Buffer#-plot:::
code::
~b_numCh1.plot;
::

note::
link::Classes/Buffer#-plot:: is not exact.
Resizing the window (especially horizontal scale) changes dynamically the wave form.
This aliasing occurs
because the display area needs to compress more sample points than available screen pixels,
causing a mismatch between the positions calculated for display and the actual displayed points.
To detailed wave form of a sound file use link::Classes/SoundFileView::.
::

section:: Using PlayBuf and BufRd

subsection:: Characteristics of Both UGens

definitionlist::
## link::Classes/PlayBuf::
|| A high-level playback link::Classes/UGen::.
It handles triggering and looping internally.
It provides a code::rate:: argument,
which sets playback speed and direction,
and a code::doneAction:: argument,
which can free the Synth automatically at the end of playback.
It is a good fit for straightforward one-shot playback and looping.
## link::Classes/BufRd::
|| A low-level buffer reader link::Classes/UGen::.
It requires an explicit phase (read-index) signal,
for example from link::Classes/Phasor::,
link::Classes/LFSaw::,
or link::Classes/Line::.
It offers precise control over playback position,
which is useful for scrubbing and phase-driven modulation.
::

subsection:: PlayBuf and BufRd Arguments

This guide focuses on the code::.ar:: methods,
because the examples produce audio output.
The signatures below show positional argument order,
but you can also pass later arguments by keyword.
The number shown after an argument name,
for example code::0:: after code::bufnum::,
is the default value.

code::
PlayBuf.ar(
	numChannels, bufnum: 0,
	rate: 1.0, trigger: 1.0, startPos: 0.0, loop: 0.0, doneAction: 0
)

BufRd.ar(
	numChannels, bufnum: 0,
	phase: 0.0, loop: 1.0, interpolation: 2
)
::

subsubsection:: Common Arguments

list::
## code::numChannels:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

Output channel count.
This value is fixed when the UGen graph is built. See link::#Compile-Time vs Run-Time: Fixed Channel Counts and bufnum::.
## code::bufnum:: (link::Classes/Buffer:: or link::Classes/Integer::)

The buffer to read from,
either a link::Classes/Buffer:: instance,
or its integer buffer index.
::

subsubsection:: PlayBuf Arguments

list::
## code::rate:: (link::Classes/Float:: or link::Classes/UGen::)

Playback speed multiplier.
Multiply by code::BufRateScale:: (code::.kr:: or code::.ir::)
to compensate for buffer and server sample-rate differences.
## code::trigger:: (link::Classes/Float:: or link::Classes/UGen::)

Restarts playback when it transitions from <= 0 to > 0.
## code::startPos:: (link::Classes/Float:: or link::Classes/UGen::)

Start position in frames.
## code::loop:: (link::Classes/Integer::, 0 or 1)

Whether to loop.
## code::doneAction:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

Action to perform when playback reaches the end,
for example code::Done.freeSelf::.
Defaults to code::Done.none::.
::

subsubsection:: BufRd Arguments

list::
## code::phase:: (link::Classes/Float:: or link::Classes/UGen::)

Read position in frames.
## code::loop:: (link::Classes/Integer::, 0 or 1)

Whether the read position wraps when it reaches the end.
## code::interpolation:: (link::Classes/Integer::)

Interpolation type.
See the class help for details.
::

note::
link::Classes/BufRd:: itself has no code::doneAction:: argument.
Instead:
list::
## The link::Classes/UGen:: driving the phase signal,
for example link::Classes/Line::,
may provide code::doneAction::.
Use that to free the server-side link::Classes/Synth::
(a link::Classes/Node::).
## If the link::Classes/UGen:: used for the code::phase:: argument does not support code::doneAction::,
use a separate link::Classes/EnvGen:: to control the playback lifetime.
::
::

subsection:: Compile-Time vs Run-Time: Fixed Channel Counts and bufnum

subsubsection:: Symptoms you may see

numberedlist::
## You get unexpected channel behavior at runtime when you try to derive
code::numChannels:: from a Synth argument:
table::
## code::
(
x = { |buf|
    PlayBuf.ar(
		buf.numChannels,
		buf
	)
};
)

x.play(args: [buf: ~b_numCh2])
::
code::
(
x = { |buf|
	var end = BufFrames.kr(buf) - 1;
	var phaser = Line.ar(0, end, 1, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf, phaser)
};
)

x.play(args: [buf: ~b_numCh2])
::
::
Both examples may play,
but post an error such as:
teletype::
Buffer UGen channel mismatch: expected 1, yet buffer has 2 channels
::
## You try to derive code::numChannels:: from a buffer argument,
and the link::Classes/SynthDef:: fails to build:
table::
## code::
(
SynthDef(\playBuf, { |out=0, numChannels=2, buf|
    Out.ar(out, PlayBuf.ar(numChannels, buf))
}).add;
)
::
::

Building the link::Classes/SynthDef:: instance fails with an error such as:

teletype::
ERROR: a PlayBuf: numChannels must be a nonzero positive integer, but received (an OutputProxy).
::

table::
## code::
(
SynthDef(\bufRd, { |out=0, numChannels=2, buf|
	var end = BufFrames.kr(buf) - 1;
	var phaser = Line.ar(0, end, 1, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	Out.ar(out, BufRd.ar(numChannels, buf, phaser))
}).add;
)
::
::

Building the link::Classes/SynthDef:: instance fails with an error such as:

teletype::
ERROR: a BufRd: numChannels must be a nonzero positive integer, but received (an OutputProxy).
::
## You call a language-side link::Classes/Buffer#-bufnum:: on a Synth argument,
and you see an error:
table::
## code::
(
x = { |buf|
    PlayBuf.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum)
};
)

x.play(args: [buf: ~b_numCh2])
::
code::
(
x = { |buf|
	var end = BufFrames.kr(buf) - 1;
	var phaser = Line.ar(0, end, 1, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, phaser)
};
)

x.play(args: [buf: ~b_numCh2])
::
::
Both examples may play,
but post an error such as:
teletype::
ERROR: Message 'bufnum' not understood.
::
::

subsubsection:: Why this happens

link::Classes/PlayBuf#*ar:: and link::Classes/BufRd#*ar:: require code::numChannels::
to be fixed when the link::Classes/SynthDef#UGen Graph Functions and Special Argument Forms#UGen graph:: is built,
that is, when the link::Classes/SynthDef:: is compiled and sent to the server.

In practice,
code::numChannels:: must be a literal positive integer,
for example code::1:: or code::2::.

table::
## Terms for Beginners:
definitionlist::
## literal integer
|| A number written directly into the graph,
for example code::1:: or code::2::.
## compile-time constant
|| A value that must be known when the UGen graph is built,
not computed later while the Synth is running.
## UGen graph
|| The fixed network of UGens and connections that defines a SynthDef.
The server runs Synth instances by executing this prebuilt graph.
::
::

When you build a link::Classes/SynthDef::
(or use code::Function#-play::,
which creates an implicit SynthDef),
the language builds a link::Classes/SynthDef#UGen Graph Functions and Special Argument Forms#UGen graph:: and sends it to the server.

At build time,
the server must already know each link::Classes/UGen::’s channel layout,
so it can allocate and connect audio buses correctly.

That is why code::buf.numChannels:: fails when code::buf:: is a Synth argument.
Inside the link::Classes/SynthDef#UGen Graph Functions and Special Argument Forms#UGen graph::,
a Synth argument is a numeric control input (a proxy),
not a language-side link::Classes/Buffer:: object.
Proxy objects do not understand language messages like code::numChannels::
or code::bufnum::,
which produces the errors shown above.

subsubsection:: Practical solutions

Use this rule of thumb:
list::
## If the buffer is known before you build the graph,
embed its channel count as a literal integer.
## If the buffer must be chosen at runtime,
use one SynthDef per channel count,
and select the SynthDef in the language.
::
Based on that rule, use one of the following approaches:

list::
## Solution A: Known buffer, embed literals in the graph

If you already have a specific buffer in the language,
the language can read link::Classes/Buffer#-numChannels::
(code::buf.numChannels:: in the example below) before building the graph,
and embed a literal value.

code::
(
var buf = ~b_numCh2;
{ PlayBuf.ar(buf.numChannels, buf) }.play;
)
::

note::
This works in any case where the Buffer is known in the language
before the UGen graph is built,
including code::Function#-play:: and explicit code::SynthDef:: construction.
It does not work when the buffer is supplied as a Synth argument.
::

## Solution B: Runtime buffer selection, use per-channel SynthDefs

If the buffer must be chosen at runtime,
prepare one link::Classes/SynthDef:: per channel count you want to support,
and select the appropriate link::Classes/SynthDef:: in the language.

code::
(
SynthDef(\playMono, { |out=0, bufnum|
	Out.ar(out, PlayBuf.ar(1, bufnum));
}).add;

SynthDef(\playStereo, { |out=0, bufnum|
	Out.ar(out, PlayBuf.ar(2, bufnum));
}).add;

~playBuf = { |buffer, out=0|
	switch(buffer.numChannels,
		1, { Synth(\playMono,   [out: out, bufnum: buffer.bufnum]) },
		2, { Synth(\playStereo, [out: out, bufnum: buffer.bufnum]) },
		{ "Unsupported channel count: %".format(buffer.numChannels).warn; }
	);
};
)
::

note::
list::
## See link::#Strategy 1: Dynamic SynthDef Generation:: for writing multiple link::Classes/SynthDef:: with iteration using link::Classes/PlayBuf:: and link::Classes/BufRd::.
## See link::#Strategy 2: Selecting SynthDefs with a Function:: for writing synth selection function with more arguments using link::Classes/PlayBuf:: and link::Classes/BufRd::.
::
::
::

note::
Passing Buffer vs bufnum as an argument

For server-side playback UGens,
the meaningful value is the integer buffer index (code::bufnum::).
In many common cases,
passing a link::Classes/Buffer:: object as an argument is also accepted,
because it can be converted to its code::bufnum::.

However,
inside a SynthDef you should treat the buffer argument as a numeric control value,
not a language object.
Do not call language messages like code::numChannels:: or code::bufnum:: on it.
::

section:: One-shot Playback

From here,
we apply the buffer and UGen basics to real playback patterns,
including reliable cleanup using code::doneAction::.

These examples emphasize server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: instance's lifetime management (code::doneAction::), which is a common source of confusion when learning one-shot buffer playback.

subsection:: PlayBuf: One-shot

A one-shot plays a buffer once and then finishes playback.

subsubsection:: Automatic cleanup version (changed doneAction: Done.freeSelf)

code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh2; // Stereo buffer.
	PlayBuf.ar(
		numChannels: 2, // Fixed channel count (stereo) inside a Function.
		bufnum: buf, // Pass the Buffer directly.
		rate: BufRateScale.kr(buf), // Normal speed (compensates for buffer/server sample-rate differences).
		doneAction: Done.freeSelf // Frees the server‑side Node instance when playback finishes.
	)
}.play;
)
::

Argument keywords are shown here for clarity.
In practice, you can omit keywords when passing arguments in documented order.
Use keywords when you want to override later arguments without providing all earlier arguments (for example, code::doneAction:: below).
code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh2; // Stereo buffer.
	PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, BufRateScale.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
}.play;
)
::

subsubsection:: Manual cleanup version (default doneAction: Done.none)

The argument code::doneAction:: defaults to code::Done.none::, so the server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: instance is not freed automatically when playback ends:

code::
/* Step 1. Optional: Press Cmd-. (or Ctrl-.) to stop any currently running nodes. */

/* Step 2. Check the current activity on the server */
s.queryAllNodes

/* Step 3. Play the function defined above */
y = { PlayBuf.ar(2, ~b_numCh2) }.play;
// Evaluate this line and check the Post window output (for example):
// -> Synth('temp__50' : 1067)

/* Step 4. Check the current activity on the server */
s.queryAllNodes
// The node created in Step 3 (for example, 1067 temp__50) is still present in the node tree.

/* Step 5. Free the node */
y.free; // Frees the node immediately.

/* Step 6. Check the current activity on the server */
s.queryAllNodes // The node created in Step 3 has now been removed.
::

subsection:: BufRd: One-shot

link::Classes/BufRd:: reads from a buffer at an explicit sample position (phase).
For a one-shot, generate a phase ramp from the first frame to the last frame and free the node when the ramp ends.

subsubsection:: Automatic cleanup version (using Line's doneAction: Done.freeSelf)

code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh2; // Stereo buffer.
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	// One-shot forward ramp: 0 -> (frames - 1) over the buffer’s duration
	var phase = Line.ar(
		start: 0,
		end: frames - 1,
		dur: BufDur.kr(buf),
		doneAction: Done.freeSelf
	);

	BufRd.ar(
		numChannels: 2, // Fixed channel count (stereo) inside a Function.
		bufnum: buf,
		phase: phase,
		loop: 0, // One-shot: do not wrap when phase exceeds the buffer range.
		interpolation: 2 // Common choice for smoother playback.
	)
}.play;
)
::

Argument keywords are shown here for clarity.
You can omit keywords when passing arguments in documented order:

code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh2; // Stereo buffer.
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	var phase = Line.ar(0, frames - 1, BufDur.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(2, buf, phase, 0) // interpolation is omitted because the default value is used.
}.play;
)
::

subsubsection:: Manual cleanup version (using Line's default doneAction: Done.none)

If you do not free the server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: instance automatically, you must free it manually.
Without code::doneAction::, the phase generator stops at the end of the ramp, but the server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: instance keeps running (typically outputting the last sample value).

code::
(
y = {
	var buf = ~b_numCh2;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	var phase = Line.ar(0, frames - 1, BufDur.kr(buf)); // no doneAction
	BufRd.ar(2, buf, phase, 0); // interpolation is omitted because the default value is used.
}.play;
)

y.free;
::

section:: Looped Playback

With link::Classes/PlayBuf:: and link::Classes/BufRd::, you can loop all or part of a link::Classes/Buffer:: instance.

Both provide a code::loop:: argument, but because the meaning and behavior differ, the code required for looping differs.

list::
## In link::Classes/PlayBuf::, the code::loop:: argument determines whether playback runs once or loops continuously.
## In link::Classes/BufRd::, the code::loop:: argument determines how the phase value is interpreted beyond the last frame index.
::

subsection:: PlayBuf: Looping

list::
## When using code::Done.none:: (code::doneAction: 0::):
list::
## Define a playback function:

code::
(
x = { |loop = 0|
	var buf = ~b_numCh2;
	var rate = BufRateScale.kr(buf);
	PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate, 1, 0, loop, Done.none);
};
::
## Test 1: Call the function with loop enabled, then release/free it:

When code::loop:: is set to code::1::, the corresponding server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: continues to run and produce sound.
It must therefore be freed manually.

code::
(
y = x.play(
	// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
	// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.
	// Also, explicitly writing default values is not required.
	target: s,             // Target server for the synth.
	// Default is nil, which refers to the local server 's'.

	outbus: 0,
	// Output bus index. Default is 0 (the first hardware output channel).
	fadeTime: 2,           // Fade‑in/out time in seconds. Default: 0.02 (20 ms).

	addAction: \addToHead, // Placement of the node in the target group.
	// Default is \addToHead, which adds the node at the head of the group.

	args: [loop: 1]        // Synth arguments; here loop is set to 1 (continuous looping).
);
)


y.release; // Triggers a fade-out of applied fadeTime (2 seconds); y.free stops immediately.
::

## Test 2: Call the function with loop disabled, then release/free it:

When code::loop:: is set to code::0::, the corresponding server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: may continue running even after playback becomes silent.
It must therefore be freed manually.

code::
/* Step 1. Optional: Press Cmd-. (or Ctrl-.) to stop any currently running nodes. */

/* Step 2. Check the current activity on the server. */
s.queryAllNodes

/* Step 3. Play the function defined above. */
// Observe what the Post window displays when evaluating the code.
y = x.play(args: [loop: 0]);
// Only required arguments need to be specified; defaults are used for others.

/* Step 4. Check the current activity on the server. */
s.queryAllNodes // The node created in Step 3 is still present in the node tree.

/* Step 5. Stop the node. */
y.release; // y.free also works, but stops immediately (no fade-out).

/* Step 6. Check the current activity on the server. */
s.queryAllNodes // The node created in Step 3 has now been removed.
::
::

## When using code::Done.freeSelf:: (code::doneAction: 2::):

list::
## Define a playback function:

code::
(
x = { |loop = 0|
	var buf = ~b_numCh2;
	var rate = BufRateScale.kr(buf);
	PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate, 1, 0, loop, Done.freeSelf);
};
)
::
## Test 1: Call the function with loop enabled:

When code::loop:: is set to code::1::, the corresponding server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: continues to run and produce sound.
It must therefore be freed manually.

code::
y = x.play(args: [loop:1])

y.release // y.free also works, but stops immediately (no fade-out).
::

## Test 2: Call the function with loop disabled: anchor::PlayBuf_loop_Test2::

When code::loop:: is set to code::0::, the corresponding server‑side link::Classes/Synth:: link::Classes/Node:: is automatically freed when link::Classes/PlayBuf:: reaches the last frame index, so there is no need to free it manually.

code::
/* Step 1. Optional: Press Cmd-. (or Ctrl-.) to stop any currently running nodes. */

/* Step 2. Check the current activity on the server. */
s.queryAllNodes

/* Step 3. Play the function defined above. */
fork { y = x.play(args: [loop: 0]); s.sync; s.queryAllNodes; }
// Evaluate this line and check the Post window output.

/* Step 4. Check the current activity on the server. */
s.queryAllNodes // The node created in Step 3 has now been removed.
::
::
::

subsubsection:: Linear rate ramp with predicted stop

When the rate modulation is defined in advance (for example, a simple envelope), you can estimate when one buffer pass will finish and schedule code::Done.freeSelf::.
This is useful when code::loop:: is enabled but you still want to stop after exactly one pass.

A buffer pass completes when the accumulated "speed multiplier" reaches one buffer duration.
In continuous form:

math::
\int_{0}^{T} r(t)\,dt = D
::

where code::r(t):: is the playback speed multiplier over time, code::D:: is the buffer duration in seconds (code::BufDur::), and code::T:: is the wall-clock time when one pass completes.

For a linear ramp from code::r0:: to code::r1:: over code::tRamp:: seconds:

list::
## If the buffer finishes during the ramp, solve the quadratic for code::T:: in: code::0.5*k*T^2 + r0*T = D::, where code::k = (r1 - r0) / tRamp::.
## Otherwise, the total time is: code::tRamp + (D - areaRamp) / r1::, where code::areaRamp = 0.5*(r0 + r1)*tRamp::.
::

code::
(
{
    var buf, r0, r1, tRamp, k, durBuf, areaRamp, finishesInRamp, tFinishInRamp, tTotal;
    var rate, sig;

    buf = ~b_numCh2;
    durBuf = BufDur.kr(buf);

    r0 = 0.25;
    r1 = 2.0;
    tRamp = 3.0;
    k = (r1 - r0) / tRamp;

    // Rate ramps from r0 to r1, then holds at r1.
    rate = EnvGen.kr(Env([r0, r1, r1], [tRamp, 1e9], \lin));

    // Buffer-time consumed during the ramp: average rate * time.
    areaRamp = ((r0 + r1) * 0.5) * tRamp;

    finishesInRamp = areaRamp >= durBuf;

    // Solve: 0.5*k*T^2 + r0*T - durBuf = 0 (only when finishesInRamp is true).
    tFinishInRamp = (
        ((-1 * r0) + (r0.squared + (2 * k * durBuf)).sqrt)
        / k.max(1e-9)
    );

    tTotal = Select.kr(finishesInRamp, [
        // Finishes after the ramp: ramp time + remaining time at r1.
        tRamp + ((durBuf - areaRamp).max(0) / r1.max(1e-9)),

        // Finishes during the ramp.
        tFinishInRamp
    ]);

    // Stop after one predicted buffer pass (even though loop is enabled).
    Line.kr(0, 1, tTotal, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);

    sig = PlayBuf.ar(
        numChannels: 2,
        bufnum: buf,
        rate: rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf), // Use .ir when buf is constant.
        loop: 1,
        doneAction: Done.none
    );

    sig * 0.2;
}.play;
)
::

subsection:: BufRd: Looping

list::
## code::loop: 1::

In the following example, in the second half—where the phase exceeds the last frame index—playback wraps to the beginning because code::loop: 1:: wraps indices beyond the last frame index.
As a result, you hear the entire buffer twice.

code::
(
x = {
	var buf = ~b_numCh1;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var duration = frames / BufSampleRate.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var phase = Line.ar(0, (frames - 1) * 2, duration * 2, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf, phase, /*loop: */1) ! 2;
}.play;
)
::

## code::loop: 0::

In the following example, the link::Classes/Node:: will be freed when link::Classes/Line:: reaches the end value, but in the second half—where its output value exceeds the last frame index—no sound is produced, since code::loop: 0:: does not wrap frame indices beyond the last frame index:

code::
(
{
	{
		var buf = ~b_numCh1;
		var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);
		var duration = frames / BufSampleRate.kr(buf.bufnum);
		var phase = Line.ar(0, (frames - 1) * 2, duration * 2, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);

		/* Opens a Server window showing the node tree. Watch for when the node disappears. */
		s.plotTree;

		BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf, phase, /* loop: */ 0) ! 2
	}.play;
}.fork(AppClock);
)
::
::


section:: Playback Rate: Pitch and Duration

Playback rate affects both pitch and playback duration.
For example, consider a sound file containing a one‑second 440 Hz sine wave:
table::
## Sound-file format || Playback rate (sample rate: 44,100 Hz; rate = 1, no scaling) || Length || Frequency
## 44,100 Hz || 2 (reads 44,100 samples in 0.5 seconds; twice as many samples per unit time) || math::\frac{1}{2}:: sec || 880 Hz
## 44,100 Hz || 1 (reads 44,100 samples in 1 second) || 1 sec || 440 Hz
## 44,100 Hz || math::\frac{1}{2}:: (reads 44,100 samples in 2 seconds; half as many samples per unit time) || 2 sec || 220 Hz
::
When changing playback rate, you must also consider the sample rate of the sound file (the buffer’s sample rate) and the server’s sample rate.
For example, consider a one‑second 440 Hz sine wave stored in a sound file at various sample rates:

table::
## Sound-file format || Playback rate (server sample rate: 44,100 Hz; rate = 1, no scaling) || Length || Frequency
## 88,200 Hz || 1 (reads 44,100 buffer frames per second; half the original speed) || 2 sec || 220 Hz
## 44,100 Hz || 1 (reads 44,100 buffer frames per second; original speed) || 1 sec || 440 Hz
## 22,050 Hz || 1 (reads 44,100 buffer frames per second; twice the original speed) || math::\frac{1}{2}:: sec || 880 Hz
::

link::Classes/Buffer#-sampleRate:: (client-side) and link::Classes/BufRateScale:: (server-side) can be used to compensate for sample-rate differences between the buffer and the server.

Another consideration is strong::aliasing:: (when speeding up) and strong::sub‑audible/low‑frequency artifacts:: (when slowing down):

list::
## When the playback rate increases, all frequency components shift upward by the same factor.
Components that move above the Nyquist frequency fold back into the audible band.
For example, at a 44.1 kHz sample rate (Nyquist = 22.05 kHz), an 8,525 Hz sinusoidal tone becomes 34,100 Hz when played at rate = 4.
Since this exceeds Nyquist, it aliases to teletype::44,100 − 34,100 = 10,000 Hz::.
As a result, instead of the intended 34.1 kHz component, you may hear an unintended ~10 kHz tone.
## When the playback rate decreases, content is shifted toward lower frequencies.
Very low‑frequency energy (including rumble or DC offset) can become more prominent and may be felt as vibration or cause modulation effects.
For example, 38 Hz becomes 19 Hz at teletype::rate = 0.5::.
::

note::
The examples in the following subsections focus on playback-rate scaling (including buffer/server sample-rate conversion) only.
They intentionally omit artifact-mitigation techniques such as anti-alias filtering, oversampling, or DC/rumble removal.
::

subsection:: PlayBuf: Rate Control

note::
Negative rates (reverse playback) are covered in link::#Reverse Playback::.
::

link::Classes/PlayBuf:: provides a code::rate:: argument. Treat code::rate:: as a speed multiplier (code::1.0:: plays at normal speed).
For correct pitch and timing when the buffer’s sample rate differs from the server’s sample rate, multiply by code::BufRateScale:: instance.

subsubsection:: PlayBuf: Static Rate

code::
(
{
	var buf, rate;

	buf = ~b_numCh2;

	rate = 1;
	// Change this to 16, 8, 4, 2, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, or 1/16.
	// Negative rates are covered in the reverse-playback section.

	/* Convert "rate relative to the buffer" into "server-rate-corrected" playback speed. */
	rate = rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf);

	PlayBuf.ar(
		// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
		// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.
		numChannels: 2,           // Fixed channel count (stereo) inside a Function.
		bufnum: buf,
		rate: rate,               // Positive = forward playback.
		trigger: 1,
		// Starts immediately since trigger causes a jump to the startPos.
		startPos: 0,              // Begins at the first frame.
		loop: 0,                  // 0 = one-shot, 1 = loop.
		doneAction: Done.freeSelf // Frees the synth when playback ends.
	)
}.play;
)
::

subsubsection:: PlayBuf: Varying Rate

To vary playback speed with link::Classes/PlayBuf::, modulate the code::rate:: argument.
Typical modulators include link::Classes/SinOsc::, link::Classes/LFNoise0::, link::Classes/LFNoise1::, and link::Classes/LFNoise2::.

list::
## For one-shot playback (code::loop: 0::), code::PlayBuf:: can free the synth automatically when the end of the buffer is reached.
This is robust even when the playback duration is not predictable in advance.

list::
## Keep the effective rate positive for forward one-shot playback.
If the modulator can cross 0, clamp it (for example, code::.max(0.0001)::).
## Use code::doneAction: Done.freeSelf:: for automatic cleanup.
::

code::
(
{
    var buf, rate;

    buf = ~b_stereo_duped;

    // Buffer-relative rate modulated by a sine wave (always positive).
    rate = SinOsc.kr(1/4).range(1/4, 1) * BufRateScale.kr(buf);

    PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate, doneAction: Done.freeSelf) * 0.2;
}.play;
)
::

warning::
If the buffer is too short, link::Classes/PlayBuf:: might not be so dramatic in changing pitch and duration:

code::
(
{
    var buf, rate;
    buf = ~b_numCh2;
    rate = SinOsc.kr(1/4).range(1/4, 1) * BufRateScale.kr(buf);
    PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate, doneAction: Done.freeSelf) * 0.2;
}.play;
)
::

Use instead link::Classes/BufRd::.
::

## If the rate modulation is deterministic and you want to stop after exactly one buffer pass even while looping (code::loop: 1::), you can predict the end time from the integral of the rate and schedule code::Done.freeSelf::.
This is mainly useful for modulation defined by an envelope (for example, a linear ramp), not for noise link::Classes/UGen::s .
See an link::#Linear rate ramp with predicted stop::.
::


subsection:: BufRd: Rate Control

Since link::Classes/BufRd:: reads from a buffer at an explicit frame index (code::phase::),  playback speed is therefore controlled by how quickly the phase value advances.

In practice:

list::
## For a constant playback rate, use a phase ramp (for example, link::Classes/Phasor::) with a constant increment.
## For a varying playback rate, modulate the increment (or otherwise generate a non-linear phase signal).
## Use code::BufRateScale.kr(bufnum):: (or code::BufRateScale.ir(bufnum):: when
## code::bufnum:: is constant) to compensate for buffer/server sample-rate differences.
## Because code::BufRd:: has no built-in end detection, decide how the node will stop (for example, schedule code::Done.freeSelf::, or free on phase wrap/boundary crossing).
::

subsubsection:: BufRd: Static Rate

code::
(
{
	var buf, rate, increment, numFrames, phase;

	buf = ~b_numCh2;

	rate = 1;
	// Change this to 16, 8, 4, 2, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, or 1/16.
	// Negative rates are covered in the reverse-playback section.

	/* Convert "rate relative to the buffer" into "server-rate-corrected" playback speed. */
	increment = rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf);
	numFrames = BufFrames.kr(buf);

	/* Buffer frame positions (phase). */
	phase = Phasor.ar(
		// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
		// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.
		trig: 0,
		// Reset position when triggered (resetPos = 0 starts at the first frame).
		rate: increment,   // Playback increment in frames per sample (scaled).
		start: 0,          // The start position of the ramp.
		end: numFrames - 1 // The end point of the ramp.
	);

	/* Automatically free the server‑side Node instance when the phase reaches the last frame
	   index. */
	Line.ar(0, numFrames - 1, BufDur.kr(buf) / rate.abs.max(0.0001), 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
	// Used only to schedule Done.freeSelf; it does not need to be routed to audio.

	BufRd.ar(
		// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
		// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.
		numChannels: 2, // Fixed channel count (stereo) inside a Function.
		bufnum: buf,
		phase: phase // Buffer frame index to read.
	);
}.play;
)
::

subsubsection:: BufRd: Varying Rate

To vary playback speed with link::Classes/BufRd::, modulate the phase increment.
Typical modulators include link::Classes/SinOsc::, link::Classes/LFNoise0::, link::Classes/LFNoise1::, and link::Classes/LFNoise2::.

There are two practical approaches:

list::
## Exact stop (recommended): free the node when the phase reaches (or wraps past) the last frame index.
This works even when the playback duration is not predictable in advance.
## Predicted stop (limited but useful): if the rate modulation is deterministic (for example, a known linear ramp), you can compute the total playback time from the integral of the rate and free the node at the predicted time.
::

list::
## strong::Exact stop by detecting wrap::

This example assumes a positive (forward) rate.
It frees the node when the phase wraps from the last frame back to the start.

code::
(
{
    var buf, numFrames, rate, increment, phase, wrapped;

    buf = ~b_numCh2;
    numFrames = BufFrames.kr(buf);

    // Buffer-relative rate, modulated by a sine wave.
    // Keep it positive to avoid reverse playback in this example.
    rate = (SinOsc.kr(1 / 10)).range(1/10, 1);

    // Convert the speed multiplier into a per-sample phase increment (frames per sample).
    increment = rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf);

    phase = Phasor.ar(0, increment, 0, numFrames - 1);

    // Detect wrap (phase jumps from end back to start).
    wrapped = phase < Delay1.ar(phase);

    // Free on the first wrap. (The wrapped signal is audio-rate, so convert it to
    // control-rate.)
    FreeSelf.kr(A2K.kr(wrapped));

    BufRd.ar(2, buf, phase, 0);
}.play;
)
::

## strong::Predicted stop for a linear rate ramp::

If the rate modulation is known in advance, you can predict when the read position will reach the end of the buffer and schedule code::Done.freeSelf::.

Idea: code::rate(t):: tells you how fast you move through the buffer (in “buffer-seconds per second”).
The buffer finishes when the accumulated buffer-time equals the buffer duration:

math::
\int_{0}^{T} r(t)\,dt = D
::

where code::D:: is the buffer duration (code::BufDur::) and code::T:: is the wall-clock time when playback ends.

For a linear ramp from code::r0:: to code::r1:: over code::tRamp:: seconds (with code::k = (r1 - r0) / tRamp::):

list::
## If the buffer finishes during the ramp, solve the quadratic for code::T:::
math::
\tfrac{1}{2}kT^{2} + r_{0}T = D
::
## Otherwise, total time is:

code::tRamp + (D - areaRamp) / r1::, where code::areaRamp = (r0 + r1) / 2 * tRamp::
::

code::
(
{
    var buf,
        frames,
        r0,
        r1,
        tRamp,
        rate,
        durBuf,
        areaRamp,
        finishesInRamp,
        tFinishInRamp,
        tTotal;
    var increment, phase;

    buf = ~b_numCh2;
    frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
    durBuf = BufDur.kr(buf);

    // Define a deterministic rate ramp.
    r0 = 0.25;
    r1 = 2.0;
    tRamp = 3.0;

    // Rate envelope (speed multiplier), then hold at r1.
    rate = EnvGen.kr(Env([r0, r1, r1], [tRamp, 1e9], \lin));

    // Area under the ramp portion: average rate * time.
    areaRamp = ((r0 + r1) * 0.5) * tRamp;

    finishesInRamp = areaRamp >= durBuf;

    // Solve: r0*t + 0.5*(r1-r0)/tRamp * t^2 = durBuf
    // Only used when finishesInRamp is true.
    tFinishInRamp = (
        ((-1 * r0) + ((r0 * r0) + (2 * (r1 - r0) / tRamp * durBuf)).sqrt)
        / ((r1 - r0) / tRamp).max(1e-9)
    );

    tTotal = Select.kr(finishesInRamp, [
        // Finishes after the ramp: ramp time + remaining time at r1.
        tRamp + ((durBuf - areaRamp).max(0) / r1.max(1e-9)),

        // Finishes during the ramp.
        tFinishInRamp
    ]);

    // Schedule node free at the predicted end time.
    Line.kr(0, 1, tTotal, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);

    // Drive phase with the varying rate.
    increment = rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf);
    phase = Phasor.ar(0, increment, 0, frames - 1);

    BufRd.ar(2, buf, phase, loop: 0, interpolation: 2) * 0.2;
}.play;
)
::
::

section:: Reverse Playback

subsection:: PlayBuf: Reverse Playback

To play in reverse, use a negative code::rate::.
Playback behavior at buffer boundaries depends on code::loop:: and code::doneAction:::

list::
## code::loop: 0:: (loop off):

code::startPos:: must be set near the end of the buffer;
otherwise, playback may stop immediately when it reaches the end of the buffer frame index.

list::
## code::doneAction:: is set to code::Done.none:: (teletype::doneAction:: number: code::0::):

The synth does not free itself automatically when playback reaches a boundary.
In this case, you can start reverse playback at the last frame.
However, you must manually call code::.free:: (or code::.release::) on the link::Classes/Node::, since it continues to run on the server.

code::
(
x = {
	var buf, rate, startPos;
	buf = ~b_numCh1;
	rate = -1 * BufRateScale.kr(buf.bufnum); // reverse
	startPos = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum) - 1;
	PlayBuf.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, rate, 1, startPos) ! 2
}.play;
)

x.free // Done.none does not free the synth automatically; free (or release) it manually.
::

note::
link::Classes/BufFrames#*kr:: is used to compute positions inside link::Classes/SynthDef::s at control rate.
code::BufFrames.kr(~b_numCh1.bufnum)::'s sclang-side equivalent is code::~b_numCh1.numFrames::.
::

## code::Done.freeSelf:: (code::doneAction:: number: code::2::):

Choose a start position before the final frame so there is data to read when code::rate < 0::.
code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh1;
	var rate = -1 * BufRateScale.kr(buf.bufnum); // reverse.
	var startPos = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum) - 2;
	// Use a safe offset (avoid reading past the end).
	PlayBuf.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, rate, 1, startPos, 0, Done.freeSelf) ! 2
}.play;
)
::
::
## code::loop:: is code::1:: (on):

Playback wraps at buffer boundaries and reverse playback continues indefinitely, so the teletype::startPos:: is less critical, but starting at the last frame is typical.
Also, code::Done.freeSelf:: does not free at the buffer boundary.

code::
(
x = {
	var buf = ~b_mono;
	var rate = -1 * BufRateScale.kr(buf.bufnum);   // reverse
	var startPos =   BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum) - 1; // or 0
	PlayBuf.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, rate, 1, startPos, 1,

		/* doneAction does not affect: */
		Done.freeSelf
		// doneAction: Done.none
	) ! 2
}.play;
)

x.free
::
::

subsection:: BufRd: Reverse Playback

With link::Classes/BufRd::, the code::phase:: argument directly controls the playback direction and speed.

list::
## Using link::Classes/Line:: (One-shot Reverse):

A simple way to play a buffer once in reverse:

code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh1;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var duration = frames / BufSampleRate.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var phase = Line.ar(frames - 1, 0, duration, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, phase) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

## Using link::Classes/Phasor:: (Continuous Looping):

When the rate is negative, link::Classes/Phasor:: moves from code::end:: to code::start::.
To start at the end immediately, set code::resetPos:: to the end value.

code::
(
x = {
	var buf = ~b_mono;
	var rate = -1 * BufRateScale.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var phase = Phasor.ar(0, rate, 0, frames - 1, frames - 1);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, phase) ! 2
}.play;
)

x.free // x.release also works.
::

table::
## strong::Compare::
list::
## One-shot playback using link::Classes/Phasor:::
code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_numCh1;
	var bufRateScale = BufRateScale.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var rate = -1 * bufRateScale;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);
	var duration = (frames - 1) / (SampleRate.ir * rate.abs);
	var end = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum) - 1;
	var phase = Phasor.ar(0, rate, 0, end, end);

	/* To free synth automatically: */
	FreeSelfWhenDone.kr(Line.kr(0, 1, duration));
	// FreeSelfWhenDone.kr could be replaced by:
	// Line.kr(0, 1, duration, doneAction: Done.freeSelf)

	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, phase, 0) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

## With link::Classes/Line::, changing the playback direction is not straightforward:

code::
(
{
	var rate = -1;
	var buf = ~b_mono;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf.bufnum);

	/* Additional logic to choose start and end positions based on playback direction: */
	var start = if(rate >= 0) { 0 } { frames - 1 };
	var end = if(rate >= 0) { frames - 1 } { 0 };
	var duration = (frames - 1) / (SampleRate.ir * rate.abs * BufRateScale.kr(~b_numCh1));

	var phase = Line.ar(start, end, duration, 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(buf.numChannels, buf.bufnum, phase, 0 ) ! 2
}.play;
)
::
::
::
::

section:: Controlling Duration and Fades

In some cases, you may want playback to last for a specific amount of time, regardless of the buffer’s effective duration at the current rate.
This section explains how to:
list::
## Define an arbitrary playback duration independent of buffer size and speed.
## Use an in‑Synth (or in‑Function) envelope (for example: code::attackTime::, code::decayTime::, code::sustainLevel::, code::releaseTime::) to shape the sound itself and to guarantee the total playback time.
## When triggering link::Classes/Function::s via link::Classes/Function#-play:: (code::{ ... }.play::), distinguish between:
list::
## Internal stop (self-terminating synth):
Stop automatically from inside the synth by using an envelope plus code::doneAction::
(for example, code::EnvGen(..., doneAction: Done.freeSelf)::).
This is the most reliable way to guarantee a fixed total duration (including fades),
because the synth controls its own lifetime.
## External stop (caller-controlled):
Keep a reference and call code::.release:: or code::.free::.
If the function creates a strong::releasable:: (gate-controlled) SynthDef graph
with a code::gate:: control used by link::Classes/EnvGen::,
GraphBuilder will strong::not:: apply player-level code::fadeTime::.footnote::

note::
Here, “graph” means the SynthDef’s UGen graph,
not a language-side object.
::

See code::UGen.buildSynthDef.canReleaseSynth.not:: in

code::
GraphBuilder {
	...
	*wrapOut { arg name, func, rates, prependArgs, outClass=\Out, fadeTime;
		...
		if(fadeTime.notNil and: { UGen.buildSynthDef.canReleaseSynth.not }) {
			...
		}
	}
}
::

in line 13 in:

code::
(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "/SCClassLibrary/Common/Control/GraphBuilder.sc").openDocument
::

code::
+ SynthDef {
	canFreeSynth { ^children.canFreeSynth }
	canReleaseSynth { ^this.hasGateControl and: { this.canFreeSynth } }
}
::

See line 63 in

code::
(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "SCClassLibrary/Common/Audio/canFreeSynth.sc").openDocument
::
::
In that case, the audible fade-out is governed by the synth’s own release stage (for example, code::releaseTime::), not by code::fadeTime::.
::

See the following example:
list::
## Gate-controlled graph:
link::Classes/Node#-release:: is handled inside the link::Classes/Synth:: instance.
The code::fadeTime:: argument passed to link::Classes/Function#-play:: is not used in this case (releasable, gate-controlled graph).

code::
(
x = { |buf, gate=1, rel=3, amp=0.2|
	var env = Env.asr(0.01, 1, rel);
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate: BufRateScale.kr(buf), loop: 1);
	env = EnvGen.kr(env, gate, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	sig * env * amp
}
)

x.class // -> Function
y = x.play(fadeTime: 1.0, args: [buf: ~b_numCh2, rel: 4.0])
y.class   // typically Synth

y.release // fades according to rel (4.0), not fadeTime (1.0):
// Because this graph is gated (releasable), Function:play does not apply player-level
// fadeTime here.
::

## Fixed-duration playback:

The synth releases itself at the exact duration, including attackTime and releaseTime (works regardless of link::Classes/Function#-play:: code::fadeTime:: argument).

code::
(
{ |buf, dur = 3.0, attackTime = 0.5, releaseTime = 0.8, amp = 0.2|
	var sustainLevel = (dur - attackTime - releaseTime).max(0);
	var env = Env.linen(attackTime, sustainLevel, releaseTime);
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate: BufRateScale.kr(buf), loop: 1);
	env = EnvGen.kr(env, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	sig * env * amp
}.play(args:[buf: ~b_numCh2]);
)
::
## Real-time performance (need a link::Classes/Env#Sustained Envelope Creation Methods::):

the synth releases itself when code::gate:: becomes teletype::0:: (works regardless of fadeTime).

code::
(
x = { |buf, attackTime=0.5, sustainLevel=1, releaseTime=2.0, gate=1, amp=0.2|
	var env = Env.asr(attackTime, sustainLevel, releaseTime);
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate: BufRateScale.kr(buf), loop: 1);
	env = EnvGen.kr(env, gate, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	sig * env * amp
};
)

y = x.play(args: [buf: ~b_numCh2]);

// Later (external control):
y.release; // triggers gated release inside the synth
// or:
y.free;    // immediate stop
::
::
::

subsection:: PlayBuf: Fades and Duration Control

numberedlist::
## Define a link::Classes/Function:: to play a buffer with fades:
code::
(
x = { |buf, dur, fadeTime, attackTime = 0.01, decayTime = 0, sustainLevel = 1, releaseTime =
0.05, gate = 1, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, amp = 1, pos = 0|
	var envFixed, envGated, sig;
	dur = Select.kr(
		((dur |!=| 0) + ((loop |!=| 0) * 2)),
		[
			BufDur.kr(buf), // when dur = 0 and loop = 0

			dur,            // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 0

			inf,            // when loop = 1;
			// alternatively use a time string, e.g. "365:00:00:00".asSeconds

			dur             // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 1
		]
	);

	/* Set Envelope */

	// 1) Fixed-duration envelope (exact total time) when dur is finite.
	//    If durTarget is infinite, we use a simple fade-in ramp and no auto-stop.
	envFixed = Select.kr(
		loop |==| 0,
		[
			Env.linen(attackTime, (dur - attackTime - decayTime - releaseTime).max(0), releaseTime)
			.kr(Done.freeSelf), // dur: finite. Exact total duration.
			Env([0, 1], [attackTime.max(0.0001)])
			.kr(Done.none) // dur: infinite. Fade in once, then stay at 1.
		]
	);

	// 2) Gate envelope (for stopping early from outside).
	//    This is the ONLY thing that responds to y.release or \gate changes.
	//    It also frees the synth after release completes.
	envGated = Env.adsr(attackTime, decayTime, sustainLevel, releaseTime, 1,
	\welch).kr(Done.freeSelf, gate);

	sig = PlayBuf.ar(
		// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
		// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.

		// When wrapping PlayBuf in a Function and calling it, numChannels must be fixed.
		// So buf.numChannels cannot be used here:
		numChannels: 2,

		bufnum:     buf,                         // Use buf directly.
		// buf.bufnum would cause an error
		rate:       rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf),
		// Supports reverse playback. Pass the Buffer directly.
		trigger:    trig,
		startPos:   start,                       // Use a safe offset.
		loop:       loop,                        // 0 = one-shot, 1 = loop.
		doneAction: Done.none
		// No doneAction to prevent duplication with doneAction in Env.
	);

	sig = sig * envFixed * envGated;       // Apply the envelope.
	Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp) // Stereo balance (pan) for a 2-channel signal.
}
)
(
x = { |buf, dur, fadeTime, attackTime = 0.1, decayTime = 0, sustainLevel = 1, releaseTime =
0.05, gate = 1, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, amp = 1, pos = 0|
	var env, sig;
	dur = Select.kr(
		(dur |!=| 0) + ((loop |!=| 0) * 2),
		[
			BufDur.kr(buf), // when dur = 0 and loop = 0
			dur, // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 0
			inf, // when loop = 1; alternatively use a time string, e.g. "365:00:00:00".asSeconds
			dur // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 1
		]
	);

	/* Set Envelope */
	env = Select.kr(
		(dur |==| inf) * (loop |==| 0),
		[
			Env.linen(attackTime, (dur - attackTime - decayTime - releaseTime).max(0), releaseTime )
			.kr(Done.freeSelf), // 1) dur: finite. Exact total duration with auto-free.

			Env([0, 1], [attackTime.max(0.0001)])
			.kr(Done.none)
			// 2) dur: infinite. Fade-in once, then stay at 1. Gate ADSR handles early stop/free.
		]
	)
	* Env.adsr(attackTime, decayTime, sustainLevel, releaseTime, 1, \welch).kr(Done.freeSelf,
	gate);
	sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf), trig, start, loop, Done.none );
	sig = sig * env;
	Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp)
}
)
::
## Playback
list::
## Control in-synth (in-function) arguments:
code::
(
x.play(s, /*outbus:*/ 0, args: [
	buf: ~b_numCh2, dur: 6, attackTime: 3, releaseTime: 3,
	rate: 1.1, trig: 1, start: 0, loop: 1, amp: 0.5
]);
)

y = x.play(s, args: [buf: ~b_numCh2, loop: 1, attackTime: 3, releaseTime: 5, amp: 0.5]);
y.release;
// Release the synth, since loop = 1 without a duration runs indefinitely. y.free works
// without respecting releaseTime.

y = x.play(s, args: [buf: ~b_numCh2, loop: 1, attackTime: 3, releaseTime: 5, amp: 0.5]);
y.free;
// Free the synth without respecting releaseTime, since loop = 1 without a duration runs
// indefinitely. y.release also works.
::
## Control link::Classes/Function#-play:: code::fadeTime:: argument:

The code::fadeTime:: argument of link::Classes/Function#-play:: does not work when a link::Classes/Function:: instance has code::gate:: argument used in link::Classes/EnvGen::.

code::
y = x.play(s, fadeTime: 4, args: [buf: ~b_numCh2, loop: 1, amp: 0.5, attackTime: 1,
releaseTime: 1]);
y.release; // release the synth, since loop = 1 without a duration runs indefinitely.
// Here, y.release does not respect fadeTime because the synth has gate. y.free also works,
// but fadeTime also does not work.

y = x.play(s, fadeTime: 4, args: [buf: ~b_numCh2, loop: 1, amp: 0.5]);
y.set(\releaseTime, 2, \gate, 0);
// It releases the synth with \releaseTime, but fadeTime does not work.
::
::
::

subsection:: BufRd: Fades and Duration Control

numberedlist::
## Define a link::Classes/Function:: to play a buffer with fades:

code::
(
x = { |buf, dur, fadeIn = 0.01, fadeOut = 0.01, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, amp
= 1, pos = 0|
	var env, frames, end, phase, sig;
	dur = Select.kr(
		((dur |!=| 0) + ((loop |!=| 0) * 2)),
		[
			BufDur.kr(buf), // when dur = 0 and loop = 0

			dur,            // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 0

			inf,            // when loop = 1;
			// alternatively use a time string, e.g. "365:00:00:00".asSeconds

			dur             // when dur ≠ 0 and loop = 1
		]
	);

	// Set an envelope
	env = Env.linen(fadeIn, (dur - fadeIn - fadeOut).max(0), fadeOut).kr(Done.freeSelf);
	rate = rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf);
	frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	end = frames - 1;
	phase = Phasor.ar(0, rate, 0, end, end);
	sig = BufRd.ar(
		// Argument keywords are shown here for clarity only.
		// They are not required, since the code already uses the correct argument syntax.

		// When wrapping BufRd in a Function and calling it, numChannels must be fixed.
		// So buf.numChannels does not work anymore:
		numChannels: 1,
		bufnum: buf,    // use buf directly;
		// buf.bufnum would cause an error
		phase: phase,
		loop: 1
	);
	sig = sig * env; // apply envelope
	Pan2.ar(sig, pos, amp) // convert mono signal to stereo (two identical channels)
}
)
::

## Playback
code::
(
play(s, /*outbus:*/ 0,
	args: [buf: ~b_numCh1, dur: 4, fadeIn: 0.02, fadeOut: 0.05, rate: 1.1, loop: 1, amp: 0.5]
)
)

y = x.play(s, args: [buf: ~b_numCh1, dur: 10, fadeIn: 2, fadeOut: 2, loop: 1, amp: 0.5]);
y.free; // Free the synth before the scheduled 10-second duration ends. y.release also works.
::
::

section:: Handling Channel-Count Mismatches

If the number of channels specified in code::PlayBuf.ar(numChannels, ...):: and code::BufRd.ar(numChannels, ...):: does not match the actual buffer, the server will post a message:
teletype::
Buffer link::Classes/UGen:: channel mismatch: expected 2, yet buffer has 1 channels
::

subsection:: Strategy 1: Dynamic SynthDef Generation

A common approach is to create separate link::Classes/SynthDef::s for mono and stereo playback.
This approach is effective when you have a collection of both mono and stereo files and want to handle them with specific link::Classes/SynthDef::s, knowing the channel count of each file in advance.

note::
In contrast to link::Classes/Function#-play::, link::Classes/SynthDef:: requires explicit code in order to use code::.release::.
To avoid an overly long explanation, the use of code::.release:: is not covered here;
see link::Classes/Node#-release:: for details.
::

subsubsection:: PlayBuf (Strategy 1)

numberedlist::
## Generate link::Classes/SynthDef:: with iteration:
code::
(
[1, 2].do { |numCh|
	var name = (\playBuf_ ++ numCh ++ \chBuf).asSymbol;
	SynthDef(name, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, dur, fadeIn = 0.01,
	fadeOut = 0.01, pos = 0, amp = 1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(
			numCh,
			buf,
			rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf),
			trig,
			start,
			loop,
			Done.freeSelf
		);
		var durAdapted = Select.kr(
			((dur |!=| 0) + ((loop |!=| 0) * 2)),
			[
				BufDur.kr(buf),
				dur,
				inf, //"365: 00: 00: 00".asSeconds,
				dur
			]
		);
		var env = Env.linen(fadeIn,
		    (durAdapted - fadeIn - fadeOut).max(0),
		    fadeOut).kr(Done.freeSelf);
		sig = if(numCh == 1) { sig ! 2 } { sig };
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp) * env;
		Out.ar(out, sig);
	}).add
};
)
::
## Playback
code::
Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf: ~b_numCh1]);

Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf: ~b_mono]);

Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf: ~b_numCh1, rate: 2, pos: -1]);

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, -1/2, \start, ~b_numCh2.numFrames - 2,
\dur, 5, \loop, 1, \pos, 1]);
x.free;
// Free the synth before the scheduled 5-second duration ends. x.release does not work.

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, -1/2, \start, ~b_numCh2.numFrames - 2,
\dur, 2, \fadeIn, 1, \fadeOut, 1, \loop, 1, \pos, 1]);

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf: ~b_stereo_duped, loop: 1]);
x.free;
// Free the synth, since loop = 1 without a duration will run indefinitely. x.release does
// not work.
::
::

subsubsection:: BufRd (Strategy 1)

numberedlist::
## Generate link::Classes/SynthDef:: with iteration:

code::
(
2.do { |i|
	var numCh = i + 1;
	var name = (\bufrd_ ++ numCh ++ \chBuf).asSymbol;
	SynthDef(name, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur = 2, fadeIn = 0.01, fadeOut = 0.01, pos = 0,
	amp = 1|
		var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
		var step = BufRateScale.kr(buf) * rate;
		var phase = Phasor.ar(0, step, 0, frames - 1);
		var sig = BufRd.ar(numCh, buf, phase);
		var env = Env.linen(fadeIn, (dur - fadeIn - fadeOut).max(0), fadeOut).kr(Done.freeSelf);
		sig = if(numCh == 1) { sig ! 2 } { sig };
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp) * env;
		Out.ar(out, sig);
	}).add
};
)
::

## Playback
code::
Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh1, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6]);
Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh1, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \fadeIn, 0.1, \fadeOut, 0.5, \amp,
0.6]);

Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_mono, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6, \pos, -1]);

Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, 1, \dur, 2, \amp, 0.8]);
Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, 1, \dur, 2, \fadeIn, 1, \fadeOut, 1, \amp,
0.8]);

x = Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, [buf: ~b_stereo_duped, rate: -2, dur: 10, \fadeIn, 1, \fadeOut, 1,
amp: 0.2, \pos, 1]);
x.free
// Free the synth before the scheduled 10-second duration ends. x.release does not work.
::
::

subsection:: Strategy 2: Selecting SynthDefs with a Function

subsubsection:: PlayBuf (Strategy 2)

code::
(
~playBuf = { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, dur, fadeIn = 0.01,
fadeOut = 0.01, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var args = [
		out: out, buf: buf, rate: rate, trig: trig, start: start, loop: loop, dur: dur, fadeIn:
		fadeIn, fadeOut: fadeOut, pos: pos, amp: amp
	];
	Synth((\playBuf_ ++ buf.numChannels ++ "chBuf").asSymbol, args)
}
)

~playBuf.(buf: ~b_numCh1, rate: 1)

~playBuf.(buf: ~b_mono, rate: -1/2, start: ~b_numCh1.numFrames - 2, pos: -1, fadeIn: 1,
fadeOut: 1)

~playBuf.(buf: ~b_numCh1, rate: -2, start: ~b_numCh1.numFrames - 2, pos: 1)

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf: ~b_numCh2, loop: 1])
x.free
// Free the synth, since loop = 1 without a duration will run indefinitely. x.release does
// not work.
::

subsubsection:: BufRd (Strategy 2)

code::
(
~bufRd = { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur, fadeIn = 0.01, fadeOut = 0.01, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var args = [
		out: out, buf: buf, rate: rate, dur: dur, fadeIn: fadeIn, fadeOut: fadeOut, pos: pos, amp:
		amp
	];
	Synth((\bufrd_ ++ buf.numChannels ++ "chBuf").asSymbol, args)
}
)

~bufRd.(buf: ~b_numCh1, rate: 1, dur: 1.3, amp: 0.5)
~bufRd.(buf: ~b_numCh1, rate: 1, dur: 1.3, fadeIn: 0.3, fadeOut: 0.3, amp: 0.5)

~bufRd.(buf: ~b_mono, rate: -2, dur: 3, amp: 0.2, pos: -1)

~bufRd.(buf: ~b_numCh2, rate: -2, dur: 3, amp: 0.2)

~bufRd.(buf: ~b_stereo_duped, rate: -2, dur: 3, fadeIn: 1, fadeOut: 1, amp: 0.2, pos: 1)
::

subsection:: Strategy 3: Stereo Balancing with Buffer.readChannel and Balance2

If the channel count is unknown,
you can force all buffers to stereo at load time using link::Classes/Buffer#*readChannel:: (by duplicating, selecting, or reordering channels as needed).
Instead of creating multiple SynthDefs, you can load buffers as stereo and use link::Classes/Balance2:: for consistent balancing.

list::
## For mono files:

Duplicate the mono channel to create a two-channel (stereo) buffer.
## For stereo files:

Use the buffer as-is (or convert only if a different mapping is required).
## For multichannel files (more than 2 channels):

Read the first two channels, or explicitly select which two channels you need.
::

subsubsection:: PlayBuf (Strategy 3)

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:::
code::
(
SynthDef(\playbuf_balance2, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf), 1, start, loop, Done.freeSelf);
	sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
	Out.ar(out, sig);
}).add
);
::
## Playback
code::
Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_mono, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // warning

Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_stereo_duped, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // no warning

Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // no warning

(
Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [
	buf: ~b_stereo_duped, // no warning
	rate: -1,
	start: ~b_stereo_duped.numFrames - 2,
	// loop: 1,
	pos: 0.5
])
)
::
::

subsubsection:: BufRd (Strategy 3)

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:::

code::
(
SynthDef(\bufRd_balance2, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur = 2, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	var step = BufRateScale.kr(buf) * rate;
	var phase = Phasor.ar(0, step, 0, frames);
	var sig = BufRd.ar(2, buf, phase);
	Line.ar(0, 1, dur, 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
	// used only to schedule Done.freeSelf (no need to multiply into audio)
	sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
	Out.ar(out, sig);
}).add
)
::

## Playback
code::
Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_numCh1, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6]); // warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_stereo_duped, \rate, 2, \dur, 3, \amp, 0.2]); // no warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_numCh2, \rate, 1, \dur, 3, \amp, 0.8]); // no warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [
    \buf,  ~b_stereo_duped,
    \rate, -2,
    \dur,  ~b_stereo_duped.duration / 2,
    \amp,  0.2
]);
// no warning
::
::

subsection:: Strategy 4: Downmixing Multichannel Output with Splay

When link::Classes/PlayBuf:: or link::Classes/BufRd:: plays a buffer with more than 2 channels, it outputs a multichannel signal.
Use link::Classes/Splay:: to downmix it to stereo.

subsubsection:: Preparing a Test Sound File and Buffer

numberedlist::
## Preparing an 8-channel sound file:

code::
(
var score;
~path = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "test.wav";

score = Score([
	[0.0, [\d_recv, SynthDef(\test, { |out = 0, amp=0.1|
		var freqs = Array.geom(16, 55, 1.48).scramble.reshape(8, 2);
		var env = Env.sine(5).kr(Done.freeSelf);
		var amps = { ExpRand(amp / 4 , amp) / 4 } ! 8;
		Out.ar(out, SinOsc.ar(freqs, 0, amps) * env);
	}).asBytes]],
	[0.0, [\s_new, \test, 1000, 0, 0]],
	[5.1, [\c_set, 0, 0]]
]);

score.recordNRT(
	outputFilePath: ~path,
	sampleRate: 44100,
	headerFormat: "WAV",
	sampleFormat: "int16",
	options: ServerOptions.new.numOutputBusChannels_(8)
);
)
::

## Allocating a 8-channel buffer from created file:

code::
~b_8chs = Buffer.read(s, ~path)
::Plotting the 8-channel buffer ~b_8chs :

## Plotting the 8-channel buffer code::~b_8chs:: to confirm the channel count:
code::
~b_8chs.plot
::
::

subsubsection:: Downmixing with Splay (PlayBuf)

code::
(
{
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(
		~b_8chs.numChannels,
		~b_8chs.bufnum,
		1 * BufRateScale.kr(~b_8chs),
		doneAction: Done.freeSelf
	);
	Splay.ar(sig) // Downmix 8 channels to stereo
}.play
)
::

subsubsection:: Downmixing with Splay (BufRd)

code::
(
x = {
	var phase = Phasor.ar(
		0,
		1 * BufRateScale.kr(~b_8chs),
		0,
		BufFrames.kr(~b_8chs) - 1
	);
	var sig = BufRd.ar(~b_8chs.numChannels, ~b_8chs.bufnum, phase, 0);
	Splay.ar(sig) // Automatically spreads channels across the stereo field.

}.play;
)

x.free
::

section:: Managing Buffer Banks

To load an entire directory of sound files into an link::Classes/Array::, link::Classes/SoundFile#*collectIntoBuffers:: is efficient.
To load an entire directory containing sound files into an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::, there is currently no equivalent method, so you must write code to do this.

To load sounds from a directory tree, recursive functions are effective.
This approach helps you organize buffers by directory structure.

subsection:: Creating Buffer Arrays from a Directory

subsubsection:: Creating a Buffer Array from a Directory: Supported Formats

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsSnd    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
)

~bufsSnd.size // -> 3

~bufsSnd[0].play
~bufsSnd[1].play
~bufsSnd[2].play
::

subsubsection:: Creating a Buffer Array from a Directory: Specific Formats

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsAIF    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*.aiff", s);
)

~bufsAIF.size // -> 2

~bufsAIF[0].play
~bufsAIF[1].play
::

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsWAV    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*.wav", s);
)

~bufsWAV.size // -> 1

~bufsWAV[0].play
::

subsection:: Creating a Buffer Bank from a Directory Tree

subsubsection:: Preparing Test Sound Files

code::
(
var baseDir = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
var sineDir = baseDir +/+ "sine";
var sawDir  = baseDir +/+ "triangle";
var renderNRT = { |defName, defFunc, outPath, dur, freq|
	var score;

	score = Score([
		[0.0, [\d_recv, SynthDef(defName, defFunc).asBytes]],
		[0.0, [\s_new, defName, 1000, 0, 0, \freq, freq, \dur, dur]],
		[dur + 0.1, [\c_set, 0, 0]]
	]);

	score.recordNRT(
		outputFilePath: outPath,
		sampleRate: 44100,
		headerFormat: "WAV",
		sampleFormat: "int16",
		options: ServerOptions.new.numOutputBusChannels_(2)
	);
};

[baseDir, sineDir, sawDir].do { |path|
	var file;
	path.mkdir;
	file = File((path +/+ "readme.txt").standardizePath, "w");
	file.write("Test text file (used to check behavior with non-sound files).\n");
	file.close;
};

(60..71).do { |midiNote|
	var freq = midiNote.midicps;

	// 1) sine/percussive, 1 second
	renderNRT.(
		\sinPerc,
		{ |out = 0, amp=0.1, freq=440, dur=1|
			var env = Env.perc(0.001, dur - 0.001, 1, curve: -4).kr(Done.freeSelf);
			var sig = SinOsc.ar(freq) * amp * env;
			Out.ar(out, sig ! 2);
		},
		sineDir +/+ ("sPerc" ++ m ++ ".wav"),
		1.0,
		freq
	);

	// 2) saw/pad (sine envelope), 2 seconds
	renderNRT.(
		\triPad,
		{ |out = 0, amp=0.1, freq=440, dur=2|
			var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.sine(dur), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
			var sig = LFTri.ar(freq) * amp * env;
			Out.ar(out, sig ! 2);
		},
		sawDir +/+ ("tPad" ++ midiNote ++ ".wav"),
		2.0,
		freq
	);
}
)
::

subsubsection:: Creating Buffer Arrays (Recursive Loading Examples)

list::
## Create buffer arrays using recursive loading (hierarchical)

numberedlist::
## Defining a function:

code::
(
~loadBuffersToArray = { |path|
	var array = [];
	var files = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
	if(files.notEmpty) {
		array = array.add(files)
	};

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = array ++ ~loadBuffersToArray.(subpath);
		};
	};
	array
};
)
::
## Test:
code::
~bufferArrayHierarchy = ~loadBuffersToArray.(Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+
"soundSamples")

/* Wait a while until buffers are allocated on server. There will be no compeleton
   message. */

~bufferArrayHierarchy[0] // -> [Buffer(...
~bufferArrayHierarchy[1] // -> [Buffer(...

~bufferArrayHierarchy[0][0].play
~bufferArrayHierarchy[0][11].play

fork { ~bufferArrayHierarchy[0].do { |buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }

fork { ~bufferArrayHierarchy[1].do { |buffer| buffer.play; 0.1.wait } }
::
::
## Create buffer arrays using recursive loading (flat)

numberedlist::
## Defining a function:

code::
(
~makeBufferArray = { |path, array|
	if(array.isNil) { array = [] };

	array = array ++ SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = ~makeBufferArray.(subpath, array);
		};
	};

	array
};
)
::
## Test:
code::
~bufferArray = ~makeBufferArray.(Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples")

/* Wait a while until buffers are allocated on server. There will be no compeleton
   message. */

~bufferArray.size // -> 24

fork { ~bufferArray[0..11].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }
// a chromatic ascending scale

fork { ~bufferArray[12..23].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }
// a chromatic ascending scale
::
::
::

subsubsection:: Recursive Loading into an Event (or an IdentityDictionary)

Organizing buffers into an link::Classes/Event:: or an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: allows descriptive access (for example, code::~bufferDic[\sine][\note60]::).
This function loads sound files into link::Classes/Buffer::s and organizes them into an link::Classes/Event:: or link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: that matches the directory structure.

numberedlist::
## Defining a function:
code::
(
~loadBuffersToDict = { |path|
	var dict = ();
	// var dict = IdentityDictionary.new;

	var entries = (path +/+ "*").pathMatch;

	entries.do { |subPath|
		var symName = subPath.basename.splitext[0].asSymbol;

		if (File.type(subPath) == \directory) {
			// If it's a directory: recursive call
			var subDict = ~loadBuffersToDict.(subPath);
			if (subDict.notEmpty) {
				dict[symName] = subDict;
			};
		} {
			// Filter by common audio extensions:
			if (["wav", "aif", "aiff", "flac"].includesEqual(subPath.extension.toLower)) {
				dict[symName] = Buffer.read(s, subPath);
			};
		};
	};
	dict
}
)
::
## Test
code::
(
~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
~bufferDic = ~loadBuffersToDict.(~rootPath);
)

/* Wait a while until buffers are allocated on server. There will be no compeleton
   message. */

~bufferDic.keys

~bufferDic[\sine].keys
~bufferDic[\sine][\sPerc60].play

~bufferDic[\triangle].keys
~bufferDic[\triangle][\tPad60].play

fork { ~bufferDic[\sine].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }
// not a chromatic ascending scale

fork { ~bufferDic[\triangle].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }
// not a chromatic ascending scale

fork {
    ~bufferDic[\sine].keys.asArray.sort.collect { |key|
        ~bufferDic[\sine][key]
    }.do { |buffer|
        buffer.play;
        0.2.wait;
    }
}
// not a chromatic ascending scale

fork {
    ~bufferDic[\triangle].keys.asArray.sort.collect { |key|
        ~bufferDic[\triangle][key]
    }.do { |buffer|
        buffer.play;
        0.2.wait;
    }
}
// not a chromatic ascending scale
::
::

subsubsection:: Accessing Buffers: Arrays vs Events (or IdentityDictionaries)

list::

## link::Classes/Array:: instances are best for sequential access or when filenames follow a strict numerical order (for example, a chromatic scale).

## link::Classes/Event:: (or link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::) instances are best for descriptive naming and organizing by category (for example, teletype::\kick::, teletype::\snare::, teletype::\lead::).
::

code::
~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3]
::

code::
(
fork {
	~invention_4_BWV775_motif.do { |n|
		var offset = 1;
		~bufferArrayHierarchy[0][n + offset].play;
		~bufferArrayHierarchy[1][n + offset].play;
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

link::Classes/Event:: (or link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::) can also be convenient if buffer names include MIDI note number.
However, it is often more useful to access buffers with human‑readable names, for example:

code::
(
fork {
	~invention_4_BWV775_motif.do { |pitch, index|
		var offset = 61;
		Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [
			buf: ~bufferDic[\sine][(\sPerc ++ (pitch + offset)).asSymbol],
			amp: 0.2
		]);
		Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [
			buf: ~bufferDic[\triangle][(\tPad ++ (pitch + offset)).asSymbol],
			amp: 0.1 / (index + 1)
		]);
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

section:: Practical Examples

subsection:: Hierarchical Buffer Array
subsubsection:: s.waitForBoot-based scheduling (Hierarchical Buffer Array)

code::
(
~loadBuffersToArray = { |path|
	var array = [];
	var files = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
	if(files.notEmpty) {
		array = array.add(files)
	};

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = array ++ ~loadBuffersToArray.(subpath);
		};
	};
	array
};

s.waitForBoot {
	SynthDef(\playBuf_2chBuf, { |buf, out = 0, pan = 0, amp = 0.1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, BufRateScale.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pan, amp);
		OffsetOut.ar(out, sig)
	}).add;

	~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
	~bufferArray = ~loadBuffersToArray.(~rootPath);

	s.sync;

	~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3];
	~noteOffset = 62;
	~arrayIndexOffset = 60;

	~bufTri = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[0][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };
	~bufSin = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[1][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };

	[~bufTri, ~bufSin].flop.do { |buf|
		[
			Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf: buf[0], pan: -0.3, amp: 0.5]),
			Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf: buf[1], pan: 0.3, amp: 1])
		];
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

subsubsection:: Event (Hierarchical Buffer Array)

code::
(
~loadBuffersToArray = { |path|
	var array = [];
	var files = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
	if(files.notEmpty) {
		array = array.add(files)
	};

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = array ++ ~loadBuffersToArray.(subpath);
		};
	};
	array
};

s.waitForBoot {
	SynthDef(\playBuf_2chBuf, { |buf, out = 0, pan = 0, amp = 0.1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, BufRateScale.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pan, amp);
		OffsetOut.ar(out, sig)
	}).add;

	~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
	~bufferArray = ~loadBuffersToArray.(~rootPath);

	s.sync;

	~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3];
	~noteOffset = 62;
	~arrayIndexOffset = 60;

	~bufTri = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[0][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };
	~bufSin = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[1][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };

	[~bufTri, ~bufSin].flop.do { |buf|
		(
			instrument: \playBuf_2chBuf,
			buf: buf[0],
			dur: 0.2,
			pan: -0.5,
			amp: 0.5
		).play;
		(
			instrument: \playBuf_2chBuf,
			buf: buf[1],
			dur: 0.2,
			pan: 0.5,
			amp: 1
		).play;
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

subsubsection:: Pattern: Pbind (Hierarchical Buffer Array)

code::
(
~loadBuffersToArray = { |path|
	var array = [];
	var files = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
	if(files.notEmpty) {
		array = array.add(files)
	};

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = array ++ ~loadBuffersToArray.(subpath);
		};
	};
	array
};

s.waitForBoot {
	SynthDef(\playBuf_2chBuf, { |buf, out = 0, pan = 0, amp = 0.1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, BufRateScale.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
		sig = Pan2.ar(sig, pan, amp);
		OffsetOut.ar(out, sig)
	}).add;

	~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
	~bufferArray = ~loadBuffersToArray.(~rootPath);

	s.sync;

	~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3];
	~noteOffset = 62;
	~arrayIndexOffset = 60;

	~bufTri = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[0][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };
	~bufSin = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferArray[1][note + ~noteOffset -
	~arrayIndexOffset] };

	Pbind(
		\instrument, \playBuf_2chBuf,
		\buf, Pseq(~bufTri, 1),
		\dur, 0.2,
		\pos, -0.3,
		\amp, 0.5
	).play;
	Pbind(
		\instrument, \playBuf_2chBuf,
		\buf, Pseq(~bufSin, 1),
		\dur, 0.2,
		\pos, 0.3,
		\amp, 1
	).play;
}
)
::

subsection:: Hierarchical Buffer Event
subsubsection:: s.waitForBoot-based scheduling (Hierarchical Buffer Event)

code::
(
~loadMonoBuffersToDict = { |path|
	var dict = ();
	var entries = (path +/+ "*").pathMatch;
	entries.do { |subPath|
		var symName = subPath.basename.splitext[0].asSymbol;
		if (File.type(subPath) == \directory) {
			var subDict = ~loadMonoBuffersToDict.(subPath);
			if (subDict.notEmpty) {
				dict[symName] = subDict;
			}
		} {
			if (["wav", "aif", "aiff", "flac"].includesEqual(subPath.extension.toLower)) {
				dict[symName] = Buffer.readChannel(s, subPath, 0, -1, [0]);
			}
		}
	};
	dict
};

s.waitForBoot {
	SynthDef(\playBuf_1chBuf, { |buf, out = 0, pan = 0, amp = 0.1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(1, buf, BufRateScale.kr(buf), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
		sig = Pan2.ar(sig, pan, amp);
		OffsetOut.ar(out, sig)
	}).add;

	~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
	~bufferDic = ~loadMonoBuffersToDict.(~rootPath);

	s.sync;

	~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3];
	~offset = 62;

	~bufTri = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferDic[\triangle][(\tPad ++ (note +
	~offset)).asSymbol] };
	~bufSin = ~invention_4_BWV775_motif.collect { |note| ~bufferDic[\sine][(\sPerc ++ (note +
	~offset)).asSymbol] };

	[~bufTri, ~bufSin].flop.do { |buf|
		[
			Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf: buf[0], pan: -0.3, amp: 0.5]),
			Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf: buf[1], pan: 0.3, amp: 1])
		];
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

